{"title":"Surface of constant width","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>A curve of constant width is a simple closed curve in the plane whose width (the distance between parallel supporting lines) is the same in all directions. \nA surface of constant width (orbiform) is a convex form whose width, measured by the distance between two opposite parallel planes touching its boundary, is the same regardless of the direction of those two parallel planes. \n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Reuleaux_triangle\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Reuleaux triangle</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Reuleaux_triangle_analogue\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Reuleaux triangle analogue</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Rotation_of_the_Reuleaux_triangle\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Rotation of the Reuleaux triangle</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Rotation_of_the_Reuleaux_triangle_analogue\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Rotation of the Reuleaux triangle analogue</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Meissner_solids\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Meissner solids</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Reuleaux_triangle\">Reuleaux triangle</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Surface_of_constant_width&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Reuleaux triangle\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Reuleaux_triangle.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/f/f3/Reuleaux_triangle.png/350px-Reuleaux_triangle.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"350\" height=\"334\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/f/f3/Reuleaux_triangle.png 1.5x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The boundary of a Reuleaux triangle is a constant width curve based on an equilateral triangle.\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> be equilateral triangle. \n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/6/356ade0c1e7dad30f160bf94c214b58271040399.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overset{\\Large\\frown} {AC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"24\" /> be the arc centered at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> with radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/8/838b06eeb6650eed888e7f331fe4e4738e7b6c55.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BB&#39; = BA, \\angle ABC = 60^\\circ.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"207\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p>Arcs <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/9/c09ee130801e889810f78dae3ddae404f49fbddc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overset{\\Large\\frown} {AB}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"24\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/a/a8a1e490cfeae6c6fa7bc3603461b3cf9c54f641.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overset{\\Large\\frown} {BC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"24\" /> define similarly.\n</p><p>All points on this arcs are equidistant from the opposite vertex.\n</p><p>Distance is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/5/70544058f77031b0cdaaa3c64bbb3bcb97e7a63b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|AB|.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"45\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p><b>vladimir.shelomovskii@gmail.com, vvsss</b>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Reuleaux_triangle_analogue\">Reuleaux triangle analogue</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Surface_of_constant_width&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Reuleaux triangle analogue\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Reuleaux_triangle_analogue.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/9/9d/Reuleaux_triangle_analogue.png/350px-Reuleaux_triangle_analogue.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"350\" height=\"336\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/9/9d/Reuleaux_triangle_analogue.png/525px-Reuleaux_triangle_analogue.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/9/9d/Reuleaux_triangle_analogue.png/700px-Reuleaux_triangle_analogue.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> be the boundary of a Reuleaux triangle, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be the centroid of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/9/919b9f77a6985e760286799909be5bfe0152f40e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"65\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/d/3dd0c712059fb378f6a688a6702e204e836c56f3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overset{\\Large\\frown} {FF_0}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"27\" /> be the arc centered at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> with radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/a/35ab1f2c59a949efb66ef6c3eda2b06969c0329c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AA&#39;, \\angle FAF_0 = 60^\\circ,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"157\" height=\"19\" /> points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/1/e01b92c33f751c21e059647f1cae1227f262041c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F, A, C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"58\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/f/dff69a75cf2c1e66399d164f5feb05f1667d52f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F_0, A, B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"65\" height=\"18\" /> are collinear.\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/6/826b85cb5a4c77c2ef495c85193e202073868c43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overset{\\Large\\frown} {F_0F&#39;}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"40\" height=\"28\" /> be the arc centered at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> with radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/e/d8e812be5649e28720a73d099ef8502103b68e7c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[|AA&#39;| + |AB|,\\]\" width=\"104\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/b/eab8f236f655e3d4840b76bcc714ad7e1b0b9bc5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle F&#39;AF_0 = \\angle ABC = 60^\\circ,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"201\" height=\"19\" /> points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/1/61148401f9ced6cee1e6e042dd3609b7657e06db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F&#39;, C, B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"65\" height=\"19\" /> are collinear.\n</p><p>Similarly define arcs from point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/7/6d794967bd855edced950369620307257fee99f8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F&#39;$\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/9/f2920fa2bc40ed68149a854f8b32c09f619b0d70.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F.$\" width=\"15\" height=\"12\" />\n</p><p>The width of this curve is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/1/ae1e5b2c2624a65609f4bd1e05300d685b594d92.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[|AB| + 2|AA&#39;|.\\]\" width=\"113\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><b>vladimir.shelomovskii@gmail.com, vvsss</b>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Rotation_of_the_Reuleaux_triangle\">Rotation of the Reuleaux triangle</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Surface_of_constant_width&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Rotation of the Reuleaux triangle\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Solid_Reuleaux_triangle_rotation.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/2/2a/Solid_Reuleaux_triangle_rotation.png/430px-Solid_Reuleaux_triangle_rotation.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"430\" height=\"221\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/2/2a/Solid_Reuleaux_triangle_rotation.png/645px-Solid_Reuleaux_triangle_rotation.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/2/2a/Solid_Reuleaux_triangle_rotation.png/860px-Solid_Reuleaux_triangle_rotation.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The left part of diagram shows the curve <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> which is a Reuleaux triangle determined by the center <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and the vertex <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/0/8207b79435a4bd499da7598fb44424c8a8499238.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>The Reuleaux triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/2/1d233be83ec56ac013d078c542367b31a9c585a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB&#39;C&#39;$\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" /> is constructed by the rotation of the curve <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> around axis <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/6/276b448cbdbfbbc9ad1d767e104d7540249da9c0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AO.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"35\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>The right part of the diagram shows the surface which arose as the result of the rotation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/2/bd222166def59a3315433877917a826de6488031.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"49\" height=\"14\" /> \n</p><p><b>vladimir.shelomovskii@gmail.com, vvsss</b>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Rotation_of_the_Reuleaux_triangle_analogue\">Rotation of the Reuleaux triangle analogue</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Surface_of_constant_width&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Rotation of the Reuleaux triangle analogue\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Surface_Reuleaux_triangle_analogy_construction.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/9/94/Surface_Reuleaux_triangle_analogy_construction.png/490px-Surface_Reuleaux_triangle_analogy_construction.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"490\" height=\"167\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/9/94/Surface_Reuleaux_triangle_analogy_construction.png/735px-Surface_Reuleaux_triangle_analogy_construction.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/9/94/Surface_Reuleaux_triangle_analogy_construction.png/980px-Surface_Reuleaux_triangle_analogy_construction.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>The left part of diagram shows the curve <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> which is a Reuleaux triangle analogy determined by the center <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/b/56b4c0ef575d9ec02ec52379f2cc6f6fa28acc13.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"18\" height=\"16\" /> the vertex <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> on curve analogue.\n</p><p>Similarly, we use the Reuleaux triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/2/1d233be83ec56ac013d078c542367b31a9c585a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB&#39;C&#39;$\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" /> is constructed by the rotation of the curve <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> around axis <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c53cc93aa43cc55bea0dc2511833fe6bdda020f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AO$\" width=\"27\" height=\"13\" /> and get the second position of the  Reuleaux triangle analogue.\n</p><p>The middle part of the diagram shows part of the surface. We can see all four parts of this surface shown by different colors. \n</p><p>The right part of the diagram shows the surface which arose as the result of the rotation curve <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/0/4e037912024535666d4f5194dcf67a6231061c8e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G.$\" width=\"18\" height=\"12\" /> It is impossible see the down side of the surface (blue) in this view.\n</p><p><b>vladimir.shelomovskii@gmail.com, vvsss</b>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Meissner_solids\">Meissner solids</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Surface_of_constant_width&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Meissner solids\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Sail_two_views.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/d/df/Sail_two_views.png/550px-Sail_two_views.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"550\" height=\"266\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/d/df/Sail_two_views.png/825px-Sail_two_views.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/df/Sail_two_views.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Limon_surface.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/c/c7/Limon_surface.png/550px-Limon_surface.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"550\" height=\"272\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/c/c7/Limon_surface.png/825px-Limon_surface.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/c/c7/Limon_surface.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Meissner_solids_1_and_2.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/4/40/Meissner_solids_1_and_2.png/550px-Meissner_solids_1_and_2.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"550\" height=\"271\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/4/40/Meissner_solids_1_and_2.png/825px-Meissner_solids_1_and_2.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/4/40/Meissner_solids_1_and_2.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> be the regular tetrahedron.\n</p><p>The «sail» <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/a/a6adfd3ad27a2dddeb0af7131d73c33c86991e93.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> is the piece of sphere centered at the vertex <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>with radii <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/a/f3ac37c9fa391eebb9601bfaca8bfbfaabdf5ca1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB = AC$\" width=\"80\" height=\"13\" /> bounded by the planes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/e/37eb2948cab9f059d612a220d4114aad2f7f2260.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ACD, BCD,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"103\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/c/94c20bf9fa393cece51326bedf1595ef4640e1cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ADF,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"48\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is the mipoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/f/b3f6f7cd16a47f33bebcf3fc361eeb808b630504.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC.$\" width=\"31\" height=\"12\" /> \n</p><p>The view along line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/3/c7387ef3aa3162cea3e516216d4ed946506bd30d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DF$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> is shown at the left part of diagram.\n</p><p>Let point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/7/6e7fdcba4c3f4d3ab437031056a497b6972b49ff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E \\in  \\overset{\\Large\\frown} {AD}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"68\" height=\"25\" /> be the center of\n</p><p>arc  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/a/a8a1e490cfeae6c6fa7bc3603461b3cf9c54f641.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overset{\\Large\\frown} {BC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"24\" /> with radii <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/9/fd9d8ab2092db23a267e65f5140a2a4ab8caff25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CE = BE = AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"137\" height=\"14\" /> shown by red.\n</p><p>The set of such arcs centered at points lying on the arc <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/5/465eaf0e6ac97ec2a94924ed69c56859af83e919.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overset{\\Large\\frown} {AD}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"24\" />\n</p><p>create a part of lemon-shaped solid between arc <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/a/a8a1e490cfeae6c6fa7bc3603461b3cf9c54f641.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overset{\\Large\\frown} {BC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"24\" /> of the plane <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/a/d7ada763e99852a6e44a15d46cce3c3709a944d5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>and arc <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/a/a8a1e490cfeae6c6fa7bc3603461b3cf9c54f641.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overset{\\Large\\frown} {BC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"24\" /> of the plane <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/4/304658890e75073c098eb0be1cbb3cb965965357.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BCA,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"50\" height=\"18\" /> shown at right part of diagram.\n</p><p>There are two possibilityes to create  Meissner solids using four sails and three lemon-shaped  surfaces.\n</p><p>If lemon-shaped surfaces made triangle  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> we name this solid as first Meissner solid.\n</p><p>It is shown in left part of diagram.\n</p><p>If lemon-shaped surfaces have the common point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/3/313048c3149436615f6cd06b7f1bec83420d6a37.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(B)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"27\" height=\"18\" /> we name this solid as second Meissner solid. \n</p><p><br />\n<b>vladimir.shelomovskii@gmail.com, vvsss</b>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018111450\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.040 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.061 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 266/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 10064/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:28530:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018111450 and revision id 224883. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}